WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:06.000
Hello, and welcome to your second drink of Golden Ticket.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:12.160
This is a really great breakdown because it includes a long interview with Rainn Wilson,

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.700
and it's not just about this episode, but it was also just about how he got his role as Dwight

4
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.300
and his time playing Dwight on The Office in general.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.040
I mean, so fun to hear all of that from him.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.600
And we've been asking you to write us with your favorite moments from these episodes

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.120
over in our Second Drinks folder on OfficeLadies.com.

8
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.640
This week's winner, we need a sting.

9
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:35.780
We need a winner's sting.

10
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:36.760
Oh!

11
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.420
This week's winner, is a moment between Michael and Daryl.

12
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.420
Kate M.

13
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.360
from Providence, Rhode Island, Lauren P.

14
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.000
from Kansas City, Missouri, wrote in,

15
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.000
and they said, hi, Office Ladies.

16
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.000
One of our favorite scenes in this episode is when Michael walks up to Daryl and says,

17
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.060
hey, you idiot.

18
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.380
And Daryl says, start over.

19
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.440
And Michael says, sir.

20
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.240
I absolutely love that moment.

21
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:02.920
It makes me laugh.

22
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.440
Every single time I watch it, I often find myself saying, sir, exactly as Michael says it.

23
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.580
Steve performed that perfectly.

24
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:10.520
You know what?

25
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.280
Why don't we hear it?

26
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.800
Hey, hey, hey.

27
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.140
You idiot.

28
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.360
Start over.

29
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:19.660
Sir.

30
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.620
It is such perfect comic timing.

31
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:27.080
I know.

32
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.620
Well, we also got a letter from Robin Y.

33
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.660
in Redmond, Oregon, who said,

34
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.040
backstory, I used to work as a bridal consultant.

35
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.660
I helped brides find their wedding dresses and help them through everything having to do

36
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.360
with the dress.

37
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.520
I got very used to lots of happy emotions and not-so-happy emotions.

38
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.720
So one day, I was in between appointments and at the front desk when one of the brides I had

39
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.680
helped came storming into the shop with her dress in the bag.

40
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.340
More backstory, this bride was legendary at the shop for taking up two appointment time slots

41
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.580
because she refused to take a dress off because she was crying.

42
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.420
So I knew exactly who she was.

43
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.080
Oh, gosh.

44
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.260
So she storms in, throws her dress on the counter, and says to me,

45
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.240
you idiot.

46
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.460
And without hesitation, I said, start over.

47
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.560
Only after figuring out what the problem was and after she left did I realize that it was

48
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.880
exactly like what happened between Michael and Daryl in the Golden Ticket episode.

49
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.440
Since then, I've dealt with all sorts of people in weddings and all sorts of emotions,

50
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.520
and I can say without a doubt that the best way to respond to someone in that situation

51
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.740
is the way Daryl responds to Michael in Golden Ticket.

52
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.800
If you work in customer service, absolutely channel the calmness of Daryl.

53
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.040
Oh, that's such a great story.

54
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.500
Start over.

55
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.480
Start over.

56
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.520
I wish I could remember that in the moment.

57
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.380
Me too.

58
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:52.980
You know?

59
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.000
I always think of those things after the fact.

60
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.440
I know.

61
00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.180
Okay, so the reason Michael is confronting Daryl is because Michael hid five Golden Tickets

62
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.680
into boxes of paper, and he's mad that one company found all the tickets.

63
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.860
We got this letter from Julia S.

64
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.320
in San Jose, California.

65
00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:11.540
Julia says,

66
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.060
I am currently watching Golden Ticket, and something is bothering me.

67
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.120
The whole plot of Golden Ticket is that one company gets all of the tickets,

68
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.660
but each of the tickets were in separate boxes.

69
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.480
How would Blue Cross have seen all of the Golden Tickets at once?

70
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.700
Do they open each one when they arrive for quality check?

71
00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.040
I feel like they would stack the boxes in a supply closet and open them when needed.

72
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:38.000
I'm just wondering, is it realistic for Blue Cross to have found all of the Golden Tickets

73
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.300
all at once?

74
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.360
Okay, I thought about this, and here's what I think happened.

75
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.720
He wasn't putting the Golden Tickets into actual reams of paper, right?

76
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.300
It was just in the box.

77
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.620
So I could realistically believe that each of these boxes was going to maybe different departments,

78
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:05.460
and that department was unloading that box and putting them into a cabinet.

79
00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.780
That's what I do when I get my box of paper at home.

80
00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.520
I take the box, I open it, and I put all the reams of paper in my, you know, printer cabinet.

81
00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:15.120
Yeah.

82
00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.020
At first, I was like, you're right.

83
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:18.480
That's totally implausible.

84
00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.980
But then I kind of, like, followed my train of thought.

85
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.100
I'm like, no, I could see it.

86
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:27.940
But so basically, your theory is, for example, like an accounting department and human resources

87
00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:34.800
and IT and sales all got their box of paper, and they all opened them on the same day.

88
00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.000
They all needed paper at the exact same time.

89
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.660
I'm not even saying they needed paper.

90
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.700
I am saying that they had a place they put paper.

91
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.960
So yes, I guess I'm saying that five different departments received a box,

92
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.820
and all five of those departments were very efficient in putting away their paper immediately.

93
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:52.640
Yeah.

94
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.300
Yes.

95
00:04:53.580 --> 00:05:00.340
Well, I might call bullshit on it, but I'm also just here for the story because I love this episode.

96
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.900
You know, it's almost a bullshit card.

97
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.780
It's like bullshit card at Jason, I think.

98
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:05.260
Okay.

99
00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:10.020
Well, lady, we got a mom detective's case in our mailbag.

100
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.060
What?

101
00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.160
About this episode.

102
00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:14.860
It's from Dene M.

103
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.420
from Cherry Hill, New Jersey.

104
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.460
And here's the mystery.

105
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.920
It's called, Whose Leg Is It?

106
00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.620
I'm in already.

107
00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:24.120
Okay.

108
00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:24.740
I love it.

109
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.620
Dene says, this is one of my favorite episodes.

110
00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.840
I watch it over and over again and notice that in the super fan episode,

111
00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.980
Kevin, Pam, Jim, and Andy are sitting in the break room,

112
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.140
and there is a leg between Kevin and Pam.

113
00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.100
It looks like there's a person there, but we never see their face.

114
00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.200
Who is that?

115
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:45.640
Who is that?

116
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.600
Whose leg is it?

117
00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.940
Was there a deleted scene with someone else in the break room with them?

118
00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.900
It has been bugging me all of these years.

119
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.160
Do you know the answer to this mystery?

120
00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.120
I love your show and appreciate all of the hard work you put into making shows

121
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.760
and content for Office fans.

122
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:01.620
Thank you.

123
00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:07.500
Well, Dene, I'm excited to report this was a very easy case.

124
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.800
We're solving this one.

125
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:09.780
We are.

126
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.800
And are you ready for this?

127
00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.920
At 25 minutes and 43 seconds, I saw the leg.

128
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:16.760
I saw the leg.

129
00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.380
And after doing a little mom detective work,

130
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.760
we realized it is our camera operator sitting on a stool.

131
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.220
They would often do that.

132
00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.700
They would have this little stool.

133
00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.000
You know, we've talked about it.

134
00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.440
Well, our walls didn't move, so they'd have to like sit in the corner.

135
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.020
Yeah, they'd have to wedge in.

136
00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:39.380
So what he was doing was capturing the other angle of the scene on Dwight at the vending machine.

137
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:40.520
That's right.

138
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.480
That's exactly right.

139
00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:46.600
And as I was re-watching the scene looking for whose leg is it, I noticed something else.

140
00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.180
And it's about Dwight at the vending machine.

141
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.480
He has a line where he says,

142
00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:52.480
Dating advice?

143
00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.060
Why didn't you ask me?

144
00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:03.440
And I noticed that Rain pops the tab on his soda between saying dating advice and why didn't you ask me.

145
00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.600
And I know why he did that.

146
00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:06.940
But let's listen.

147
00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.840
I want you guys to just hear this little moment.

148
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:09.980
Wait a minute.

149
00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:10.820
Dating advice?

150
00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:12.920
Why didn't you ask me?

151
00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:14.280
Uh-huh.

152
00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:15.440
Dating advice?

153
00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.900
Why didn't you ask me?

154
00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:19.760
He did that for sound.

155
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.840
Because in film and television, you can never do anything loud on your line or else they can't cut around it.

156
00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:27.460
Mm-hmm.

157
00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:34.220
Also, in the background of that scene, when Rain first enters and he's putting the money in the vending machine and then he gets a soda,

158
00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.700
first of all, I'm convinced he's not really putting any money in there because it would have made a sound.

159
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.840
I think he's fakie putting coins in.

160
00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:48.420
And then that soda was pre-planted at the bottom of the machine so you wouldn't hear the noise of it going down.

161
00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.540
Because it would ruin the dialogue at the table.

162
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:57.400
They need to get one of those vending machines on a set that is the arm that just goes.

163
00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.020
Those are sometimes loud, though.

164
00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.240
Are they?

165
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:03.060
I don't know.

166
00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:04.780
Not as loud as like the.

167
00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.260
Oh, yeah.

168
00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:06.520
Yeah.

169
00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:07.300
The old school one.

170
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:07.640
Yeah.

171
00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.260
I love watching the arm.

172
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.120
The arm is kind of cool.

173
00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:11.580
Yeah.

174
00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:12.400
I hear you.

175
00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.820
Sometimes I don't even need anything, but I just want to watch the arm.

176
00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:22.160
Oh, so you'll be more likely to buy something out of a vending machine with an arm than a one?

177
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:22.760
Yes.

178
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:23.960
I love the arm.

179
00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.020
I don't know, guys.

180
00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.820
It's the small things in life.

181
00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:28.340
All right.

182
00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:32.180
Well, acting students out there, that was a hot tip, you know?

183
00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.500
So if you have an envelope you have to open, don't open it on your line.

184
00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.840
Don't get that soda can on your line.

185
00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.060
All the things you learn right here on Office Ladies.

186
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:42.760
Exactly.

187
00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.040
All right, you guys.

188
00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:45.820
Thanks for sending in your questions and comments.

189
00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.520
Now, please enjoy our second drink of Golden Ticket.

190
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:53.500
I'm Jenna Fisher.

191
00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:54.820
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

192
00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.080
We were on The Office together.

193
00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.360
And we're best friends.

194
00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:01.160
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

195
00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.540
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

196
00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.160
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

197
00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:09.300
We're The Office Ladies.

198
00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:15.170
Hello, everyone.

199
00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:16.090
Hi.

200
00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:21.030
Today is Golden Ticket, Season 5, Episode 19.

201
00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:25.170
This was written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Randall Einhorn.

202
00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.030
It's a big day for Michael.

203
00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.810
Oh, it's a big idea for Michael.

204
00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.530
It's a big idea day for Michael.

205
00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:33.830
Here's your summary.

206
00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:39.690
Michael decides to execute a Willy Wonka-themed promotional idea.

207
00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:46.070
He is going to take some Golden Tickets, he's going to hide them in some paper shipments,

208
00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:51.470
and any company that finds a Golden Ticket gets 10% off.

209
00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.590
And it's not limited to one.

210
00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.510
Well, we're going to find that out.

211
00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:56.850
Yeah.

212
00:09:57.250 --> 00:09:59.130
This idea is going to backfire.

213
00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:04.890
He is going to pressure Dwight into taking the fall, but then that backfires.

214
00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:05.870
Yeah.

215
00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.210
A lot of ideas and a lot of backfires.

216
00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.570
A lot of layers here, playing games.

217
00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:13.070
Mm-hmm.

218
00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:14.010
Yeah.

219
00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:20.190
Meanwhile, Pam, Jim, and Andy are all giving dating advice to Kevin.

220
00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.030
It's somewhat conflicting.

221
00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:28.370
Kevin's a little confused, but he's going to try to make a connection with Lynn from Blood Drive.

222
00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:29.010
Mm-hmm.

223
00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:31.090
From their Lonely Hearts Mixer.

224
00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:34.010
Lynn is still in the mix from the Mixer.

225
00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:34.470
Yeah.

226
00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:36.650
Let's get into fast facts.

227
00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:38.850
Fast fact number one.

228
00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:46.470
This episode contains maybe one of the most famous office GIFs of them all.

229
00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:48.050
You know what I'm talking about.

230
00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:48.790
A GIF or a GIF?

231
00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:49.550
It doesn't matter.

232
00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:50.970
You know the one we're talking about.

233
00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:52.970
Is it GIF or JIF?

234
00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:54.010
I don't know, guys.

235
00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:55.210
I've never known.

236
00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.190
I'll never know.

237
00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.570
Let's ask the two hip young people in the booth.

238
00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.230
Please, young people, tell us how to say it.

239
00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.410
I say GIF.

240
00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:03.410
What do you say?

241
00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:04.610
No, I say GIF.

242
00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:05.310
Sorry.

243
00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:08.990
I think the correct one is GIF, but it feels wrong.

244
00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:10.790
But everyone I know says GIF.

245
00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.390
It's kind of like GIF, but you take the T off.

246
00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:14.830
So I think it's GIF.

247
00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:16.770
Well, I've been saying GIF.

248
00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.230
I say GIF.

249
00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:19.310
There you have it.

250
00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:20.550
Also, what's a meme?

251
00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.010
What is a meme?

252
00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.610
A GIF is if it moves, and a meme is if it's still.

253
00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:31.090
And also, I mean, people say GIF is correct, but keep in mind, it stands for graphics interchange,

254
00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.130
whatever the F is.

255
00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.090
So it starts with a G.

256
00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.170
