00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.860
And now, Rainn, did she just walk on up?

551
00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.780
Or did, like, how did Alma get there?

552
00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:26.020
So, my wife had a horse for a long time named Gus.

553
00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:29.980
And Gus was a very old, white, liposan horse.

554
00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:31.560
And he passed away this summer.

555
00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.300
He lived a rich, full horse life.

556
00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:36.600
And he died very quickly.

557
00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:38.240
So, that was all fine.

558
00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:42.900
But his stall was empty, and they put a vase of flowers in there.

559
00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:46.820
And it was just kind of left empty as a kind of a salute to old Gus.

560
00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.360
And two weeks later, no joke.

561
00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:51.820
And Gus was white, by the way.

562
00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:59.980
Two weeks later, out of nowhere, sitting in Gus's horse stall was a white peahen.

563
00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.400
A female peacock.

564
00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:02.620
Oh, my goodness.

565
00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:03.520
Whoa.

566
00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.180
No one knows where it came from.

567
00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.040
It had never been seen there previously.

568
00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:12.140
It hadn't been seen in the neighborhood or the area of where my wife's horse, where she

569
00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:12.860
rides her horses.

570
00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.460
And it just moved in to his stall.

571
00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:20.760
And so, she kind of trained it to eat, feed.

572
00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:24.500
And it couldn't stay there because it was spooking some of the horses because it would

573
00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:25.700
flap around and squawk.

574
00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:33.800
And so, she trained it to eat, feed, and go into a carrying container bin.

575
00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:36.100
And we took it to our house.

576
00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.540
And it made the transition perfectly.

577
00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:41.260
And you guys, Alma is amazing.

578
00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:45.500
And she sleeps during the day when she's not hunting bugs.

579
00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:50.160
She sleeps in the olive tree right outside my office window and just stares in at me all

580
00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:50.360
day.

581
00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:56.300
So, if I'm, like, podcasting or on Zoom calls or whatever, I have a white peacock scaring

582
00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:57.000
into my office.

583
00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:59.380
This is all I could ever hope for, Rain.

584
00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:01.200
I'm excited about my hummingbirds.

585
00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.680
If I had a peacock in my tree, I'd lose it.

586
00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:06.620
I mean, Rain, it's so cool.

587
00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:07.740
It's Gus, right?

588
00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:12.720
My friend said if there was ever more proof needed for reincarnation.

589
00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:15.500
Now, I don't know about, I don't know that I buy reincarnation.

590
00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:17.900
But, I mean, it was magical.

591
00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:20.040
It's a magical experience.

592
00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:22.020
It's kind of crazy.

593
00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:23.800
Well, there's something happening there.

594
00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:23.960
Yeah.

595
00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:27.500
I was going to say, whether you believe in reincarnation or not, there's just something

596
00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:29.720
beautiful in the universe that happened right there.

597
00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:30.400
That's right.

598
00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:30.840
Yeah.

599
00:24:31.060 --> 00:24:35.620
So, besides now our pigs that live at our house, now we have a peacock who is very

600
00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:36.040
demanding.

601
00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:37.960
Holly, my wife, just went crazy.

602
00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:41.120
She bought, like, a big box arrived yesterday.

603
00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:43.100
And I opened it up and it was filled.

604
00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:44.180
I'm not kidding you.

605
00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:46.140
It was filled with mealworMs.

606
00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:46.480
Ew.

607
00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:53.620
Two, like, 20 pound boxes of bags of mealworMs.

608
00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:54.680
Where do you keep those?

609
00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:55.040
Yeah.

610
00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:56.500
Do you refrigerate them?

611
00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:56.840
God save us.

612
00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:58.240
Like, what do you do with the meal?

613
00:24:58.420 --> 00:24:59.660
Where do you put a mealworm?

614
00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:03.940
You just put them on the ground and then she eats the mealworMs.

615
00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:05.440
But in between eating.

616
00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:07.120
Not my wife, the peacock.

617
00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:07.720
The peacock.

618
00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:08.700
We got that part.

619
00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:11.720
Well, Rain, listen, we're so excited to have you on.

620
00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:15.480
We know you've been on before and you've sent in audio clips and you're always so supportive.

621
00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:22.520
But we realize there are just some real general questions we ask our guests and we never got

622
00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:23.400
to do that with you.

623
00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:26.220
And here is the one we always lead with.

624
00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:26.800
Are you ready?

625
00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:28.000
Okay.

626
00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:28.480
Hit me.

627
00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:29.340
All right.

