00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:03.710
and I need to talk a lot.

771
00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:07.690
You know, they think like, oh, improvisation means talking a lot.

772
00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:12.830
And this was an improv where I kind of did this monologue and you just gazed painfully

773
00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:20.310
into space, taking in my body odor and my proximity and the strangeness of my story.

774
00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:22.510
And it was, it was fantastic.

775
00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:25.750
And when we did that, I was like, oh, I know that you're going to have these parts.

776
00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:26.690
Yeah.

777
00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:27.910
I felt that way too.

778
00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:32.050
And I felt at that audition, it was very clear to me that you were their choice.

779
00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:35.590
Um, do they keep pairing different people with rain?

780
00:32:35.750 --> 00:32:37.670
Cause that's how, you know, you know, that's the point.

781
00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:41.310
When they're like, oh, let's see him with this person and this person and this person.

782
00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:43.210
And I only got paired with him once.

783
00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:47.830
And that made me a little nervous because it was very clear to me that rain had the part.

784
00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:51.830
And so I was like, oh, well, can I, I'll pair with him again.

785
00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:52.990
With a lot of different gyMs.

786
00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:55.150
I remember reading with a lot of different gyMs.

787
00:32:55.270 --> 00:32:55.450
Yeah.

788
00:32:55.450 --> 00:33:06.810
Um, and the reason they said ultimately they went with John, um, I mean, obviously he's charming and handsome and funny and great deadpan and smart as hell and likable,

789
00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:10.670
but that his size was comparable to mine.

790
00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:13.470
There were a couple other gyms that were like five foot seven.

791
00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:17.110
And it just seemed like Dwight would just overpower them.

792
00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:19.050
But Jim was actually taller than me.

793
00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:23.350
So we kind of like, we really matched up kind of energy wise.

794
00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:25.310
That makes sense to me.

795
00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:25.750
Yeah.

796
00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:26.470
That does.

797
00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:31.610
But then they went a whole other way with your romantic life and they picked the tiniest person in the office.

798
00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:35.510
The tiniest, the tiniest little sprig.

799
00:33:35.770 --> 00:33:36.170
Yeah.

800
00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:39.610
But I didn't think they knew that Dwight and Angela were going to get together.

801
00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:40.250
They just were.

802
00:33:40.750 --> 00:33:45.310
I don't know where that storyline came from, but I imagine they were just kind of floundering in season two.

803
00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:46.610
Like, oh, we got to mix things up.

804
00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:50.050
Like, because at first they had me together with Kelly.

805
00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:51.730
They had me kiss Kelly Kapoor.

806
00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:53.830
But Angela was furious.

807
00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.510
Angela watched it and was furious.

808
00:33:57.370 --> 00:33:57.770
Ah.

809
00:33:58.150 --> 00:33:59.950
Remember they had me like look in the camera.

810
00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:03.610
Like, they made me look so, like, they kept saying more anger, more.

811
00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:06.050
I was like, oh my God, my eyes are going to pop out of my head.

812
00:34:07.150 --> 00:34:09.750
But I think, I think our writers just sat there.

813
00:34:09.850 --> 00:34:15.250
Jenna and I have talked about this and we've talked to our writers about it, how they would just take two names and put them on a card.

814
00:34:15.890 --> 00:34:21.630
And just see what that felt like and sort of spitball a little bit about what those two people would bring out of each other.

815
00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:22.550
Right.

816
00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:26.090
And that is one thing that Greg talked about.

817
00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:30.410
And I remember, and it's a really great lesson for comedy writers and screenwriters.

818
00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:38.610
He said, a lot of shows really focus on a character and like, oh, let's make this character really specific and funny and interesting.

819
00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:46.350
But he was really about like, when these two characters bang up against each other, that's what she said.

820
00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:51.230
Then what do, what happens, what happens with that energy, you know?

821
00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:55.150
So it was more about, it was much more about dynamics between characters.

822
00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:57.790
And I think that's one of the successes of The Office.

823
00:34:58.250 --> 00:35:06.710
Well, this episode that we're talking about today, Golden Ticket, has a classic Michael-Dwight pairing.

824
00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:18.450
And we just, I love in this rewatch, watching the way that Dwight can be both Michael's lackey, and then also like, you can take him to task.

825
00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:19.630
You stand up to him.

826
00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:22.350
What was that like for you?

827
00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:28.550
You were in this comedy duo with Steve Carell for seven years, playing out moments like this.

828
00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:30.290
What was that experience?

829
00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:34.210
Well, that's a bunch of questions.

830
00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:35.330
And I'll let you throw it.

831
00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:37.750
That's how I like to interview people.

832
00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:43.130
She just likes to throw like 13 big, overarching, like huge questions.

833
00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:44.790
Yeah, thematic questions all at once.

834
00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:46.110
I love it.

835
00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:47.510
Well, let me hit that one.

836
00:35:47.590 --> 00:35:52.450
Let me just say that, you know, when I look back on that work with Steve,

837
00:35:52.450 --> 00:36:07.410
Steve, I'm just so grateful, because he's obviously like one of the greatest actors, comedy actors, but even just actors like ever.

838
00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:13.410
And the fact that I got to do so much work with him was just such a gift.

839
00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:16.130
I didn't come from an improv background.

840
00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:22.230
I was always good at improv, but I went to theater school, and I did Shakespeare, and I did Eugene O'Neill.

841
00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:26.950
And I was in New York, kind of off-Broadway theater guy.

842
00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:30.710
And Steve was from Second City and had done years and years and years of improv.

843
00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:33.170
So that was his thing.

844
00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:43.570
But the thing about Steve that I'll, you know, they always have this, it's kind of become a cliche, like the yes and, you know, like in improv, if you say like,

845
00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:54.450
hey, you know, I bought a pet peacock, then you say yes and, and like, oh, I'd love to see it.

846
00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:56.210
Can I feed it my mealworms or whatever?

847
00:36:56.210 --> 00:36:59.070
And Steve was incredible at that.

848
00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:04.270
No matter what I did, and I was, sometimes I would try to go really out of the box.

849
00:37:04.770 --> 00:37:12.850
Steve would completely react in character and, and accept what was being proposed.

850
00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:18.530
So I could just in the, because obviously we would, we would shoot a scene, we would improvise.

851
00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:23.230
I mean, we would do the script, we would shoot the script and make sure we got it scripted correctly.

852
00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:25.130
And then we would, could improvise.

853
00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:28.790
And then sometimes it would just, we'd just freely improvise and just go off.

854
00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:37.250
And I, I remember just being like, I could say something like, um, I decided to open a llama farm, just out of nowhere or something.

855
00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:43.310
And Steve would just listen as Michael, take that in and be like, Dwight, what are you talking about, llamas?

856
00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:45.530
What, what does that have to do with anything?

857
00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:49.870
Why, what, you're so weird, Dwight, ugh, go away, you're icky.

858
00:37:49.870 --> 00:37:53.330
Like, and it would just be completely in the moment.

859
00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:57.950
And I just learned so much from that process.

860
00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:04.730
Um, but he never got kind of like, God damn it, Rain, why are you being so weird and so silly?

861
00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:12.210
Like stay on the point of the scene and we can go way off kilter, you know, way off the path.

862
00:38:12.370 --> 00:38:15.670
And he would just be right there all the time.

863
00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:18.810
It was really a privilege to work with him.

864
00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:26.570
I loved it when there'd be a conference room scene and clearly Michael had an agenda and things to say.

865
00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:30.970
And you, you as Dwight, of all people would interject, right?

866
00:38:31.690 --> 00:38:38.650
And Rain, so many times I go to the script to see if it was written or improvised, because I feel like I can tell now.

867
00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:43.570
And they're always so good, but, but it's like seamless.

868
00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:47.650
You would never know because of exactly what you just shared, because Steve is Michael.

869
00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:52.570
No matter what you piped up and said, just like would just take it and run with it.

870
00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:56.770
And, um, it was always like an absolute joy.

871
00:38:56.950 --> 00:38:59.230
You know me, I'm a nerdy, nerdy improv gal.

872
00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:03.850
We've talked on the podcast that you and Jenna had your whole, like we did theater.

873
00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:08.070
And Jenna likes to remind me of it, Rain, much like you did.

874
00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:15.330
Um, but as like a nerdy improv gal watching you guys, uh, it was, it was just awesome.

875
00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:18.830
I felt like I had a front row seat of just some really magical moments.

876
00:39:19.290 --> 00:39:21.690
And getting specifically to Golden Ticket.

877
00:39:22.030 --> 00:39:22.650
Thank you, Angela.

878
00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:31.250
And, but yeah, I hadn't seen this one since it came out and it was so fun to watch.

879
00:39:31.510 --> 00:39:42.630
And Jenna, just like you said, that dynamic of Dwight being toady, lackey, suck up, just so desperate for Michael's approval.

880
00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:55.890
Um, and then oddly realizing he's got some power here and, you know, that switch when he's shaking David Wallace's hand and he says, you know, thank you.

881
00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:57.210
And takes the credit for it.

882
00:39:57.210 --> 00:40:03.950
And then Steve's expression of, it was so priceless of like, oh, that guy.

883
00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:12.570
And then your fight in the marketing meeting, like when you get on the phone with the marketing guys, the two of you back and forth with your big ideas.

884
00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:13.890
Yeah.

885
00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:19.610
Horse boats, um, and all the toilet, toilet, toilet nets.

886
00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:22.090
He has a lot of toilet ideas.

887
00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:24.130
Um, you don't have ideas.

888
00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:24.870
Yes, I do.

889
00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:26.730
Um, yeah.

890
00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:33.210
I would love to see an extended cut of that because I bet that there were a lot more ideas we tossed back and forth in that scene.

891
00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:39.330
We should ask Dave Rogers, the keeper of all of the extra footage, because I bet you're right.

892
00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:40.890
There had to have been even more.

893
00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:51.030
Also, this one was written by Mindy Kaling and she was amazing at coming up with on the fly, in the moment, extra stuff to hand you.

894
00:40:51.030 --> 00:40:57.350
I have to tell you guys, in the script, I reread it, Pam tries to butt in with an idea.

895
00:40:57.690 --> 00:40:58.050
What?

896
00:40:58.050 --> 00:41:02.630
Yes, and Michael and Dwight shoot her down.

897
00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:06.070
It's the one thing they agree on is that Pam's idea is so stupid.

898
00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:12.330
She says, as they're yelling all their ideas, Pam meekly just says, remote control toilets.

899
00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:14.270
And they're both like, nah, it's stupid.

900
00:41:14.370 --> 00:41:14.970
Doesn't make sense.

901
00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:15.450
It's dumb.

902
00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:19.470
I wish I would have kept that.

903
00:41:19.590 --> 00:41:20.110
That's great.

904
00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:23.650
We have another question that we've never asked you, Rain.

905
00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:27.150
This is a popular one we ask all our guests.

906
00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:27.930
Okay.

907
00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:30.890
Did you take anything from set when the show wrapped?

908
00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:33.910
Oh, this breaks my heart.

909
00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:36.950
I should have taken so much more.

910
00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:39.290
I should have fleeced NBCUniversal.

911
00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:42.550
I took Dwight's glasses.

912
00:41:42.770 --> 00:41:46.110
I took his little desk plate that said Dwight Schrute on it.

913
00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:47.970
I took his stapler.

914
00:41:50.330 --> 00:41:53.730
And one or two other things I can't quite remember.

915
00:41:54.010 --> 00:41:55.210
I have to look around and see.

916
00:41:55.850 --> 00:41:59.250
But I had a whole list of things I gave to Phil Shea.

917
00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:02.090
And I said, Phil, pull these aside for me.

918
00:42:02.710 --> 00:42:04.150
And I'll get them from you later.

919
00:42:04.850 --> 00:42:10.910
And it was, you know, Dwight's briefcase and this and that and the other thing and blah, blah, blah.

920
00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:13.850
And Phil was like, okay.

921
00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:20.180
And then I went back to Phil and he's like, I don't have them or I couldn't get them.

922
00:42:20.440 --> 00:42:21.140
Sorry, blah, blah, blah.

923
00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:22.720
And then I heard from someone else.

924
00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:34.360
They were like, oh, they threatened the crew to be fired and never worked for NBCUniversal again if they caught them taking anything from the office.

925
00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.320
And we didn't know why.

926
00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:42.800
And then they had that big auction where they auctioned all the stuff off on eBay.

927
00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:46.040
You know, even like Dwight's desk was auctioned off.

928
00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:48.240
Did you not get your bobblehead?

929
00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:54.740
I got one of the rejected bobbleheads that doesn't look anything like the bobblehead.

930
00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:56.520
I did not get my original bobblehead.

931
00:42:56.520 --> 00:42:57.640
I see.

932
00:42:58.160 --> 00:42:58.860
That's a shame.

933
00:42:58.860 --> 00:43:03.740
And I asked Phil Shea for the painting, the Dwight painting where he's like Chairman Mao.

934
00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:04.320
Yeah, yeah.

935
00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:07.700
And that didn't happen either.

936
00:43:08.180 --> 00:43:11.300
Well, Rain, you know, you've gone on to do so many awesome things.

937
00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:15.860
But we would love to talk to you about your podcast, Metaphysical Milkshake.

938
00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:20.900
I listened to a great episode last night when you had Mike Schur on.

939
00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:22.140
Yeah.

940
00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:26.580
And that was so amazing just talking about what is good, what is bad.

941
00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:30.060
And he sort of like folded in his show, The Good Place, into that episode.

942
00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:32.280
But you and Reza are so thoughtful.

943
00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:35.200
And you're really asking life's big questions.

944
00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:36.960
And it's a great listen.

945
00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:39.220
Yeah, thanks.

946
00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:43.100
Well, you guys know this is something that's always been really important to me.

947
00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:53.300
And it was kind of the impetus and inspiration for starting SoulPancake, the digital media company that I created with some friends.

948
00:43:53.300 --> 00:43:58.380
But this whole idea of life's biggest possible questions has always really intrigued me.

949
00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:00.800
You know, I took philosophy in college.

950
00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:03.920
And I love, you know, studying spirituality.

951
00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:11.740
And I love, you know, psychology and sociology and kind of creativity and how creativity works and why.

952
00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:17.320
And so this was kind of the foundation for Metaphysical Milkshake.

953
00:44:17.840 --> 00:44:22.560
Our first season we did on this company called Luminary, but that didn't really work out.

954
00:44:22.660 --> 00:44:30.080
So now we're doing a second season, which is available wherever podcasts are available and including a lot of the stuff we did for our first season.

955
00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:32.480
And it's super fun.

956
00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:36.880
We get to talk to just artists and thinkers and writers.

957
00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:50.920
We just yesterday interviewed Avi Loeb, who's the head of the astronomy division of Harvard University, talking about alien life and discovering extraterrestrial life.

958
00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:52.360
And and then we had Jason.

959
00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:58.440
Speaking of office auctions, Jason Isbell is one of my favorite kind of country rock.

960
00:44:58.580 --> 00:44:59.660
I love him.

961
00:45:00.080 --> 00:45:01.300
Yeah, he's so great.

962
00:45:01.480 --> 00:45:06.380
Yeah, he came on the show talking about like what music is like, where does music come from?

963
00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:07.980
Oh, I need to listen to that one.

964
00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:10.380
He bought years ago.

965
00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:15.120
He bought his wife's white scissors from the NBC auction.

966
00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:16.720
Whoa.

967
00:45:18.340 --> 00:45:19.640
Oh, isn't that crazy?

968
00:45:19.820 --> 00:45:20.120
Yes.

969
00:45:21.260 --> 00:45:26.280
Well, Rain, I listened to your interview with Adam Grant, where you discussed this concept.

970
00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:27.980
What if I'm wrong?

971
00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:28.980
What if I'm wrong?

972
00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:34.320
And this idea, our resistance as people to consider that you might be wrong.

973
00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:41.840
Or even what I loved when you guys talked about how when you know you're wrong, but the cost of admitting you're wrong.

974
00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:47.400
That idea of like, there's that social coSt.

975
00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:53.040
Oh, no, if I admit I'm wrong once, people will paint me with a brush that, oh, that's the person who gets things wrong.

976
00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:55.600
I mean, it was just, it was so thoughtful.

977
00:45:55.940 --> 00:46:05.000
I love that kind of introspective discussion that makes me think about things that maybe I wouldn't have thought of otherwise.

978
00:46:05.660 --> 00:46:06.880
It's a great podcaSt.

979
00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:12.080
You know, I'm glad you brought that one up because I feel like this is kind of where our country is.

980
00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:17.040
It's very stuck in kind of two sides that are convinced they're 100% right.

981
00:46:17.160 --> 00:46:21.560
And no one's really talking to each other and learning and listening from each other.

982
00:46:21.740 --> 00:46:28.780
And when you go to life's biggest questions, these are the questions that we've been asking since the caveman days, since ancient Greece.

983
00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:31.640
You know, why are we alive?

984
00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:32.920
Do we have free will?

985
00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:34.140
What's the meaning of it all?

986
00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:38.600
And this is a, these discussions can unite us, you know?

987
00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:49.040
So that's really the whole purpose is to unite people by getting young folk thinking about talking, wrestling with, you know, the human experience.

988
00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:55.300
Well, Rain, at the end of your podcast with Reza, you guys do this sort of rapid fire of questions.

989
00:46:55.820 --> 00:47:01.040
And I love when you ask people, when do you most feel connected to the universe?

990
00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:04.440
I'm paraphrasing, but it's something like that question, right?

991
00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.500
So when do you, Rain, feel most connected to the universe?

992
00:47:10.180 --> 00:47:11.320
Wow, you're okay.

993
00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:15.580
You're hitting me with my own weapon.

994
00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:16.600
That's what she said.

995
00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:20.180
That's a profound question.

996
00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:22.300
And wow, no one's ever asked me that before.

997
00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:25.120
